albor provides C++ targets for the declarative UI framework:

    find_package(albor CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE albor)          # recommended GPU runtime (Metal / Direct3D)

The portable CPU renderer arrives through the package feature `albor[blend2d]` on macOS and
Windows; the Linux package carries it by default until the Vulkan backend lands (#110):

    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE albor-blend2d)  # the blend2d feature's umbrella

Compile a .ui component at build time:

    albor_build_ui_component(main "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/panel.ui")

The public headers install under an `albor/` folder, so consumer sources include
`#include "albor/application/application.h"` and the paths match the `albor::` namespace.

Exactly one platform adapter, one text engine and one renderer may be linked; the umbrella
target carries them all, and the granular albor-*-auto targets are the expert surface.

On Windows the consumer's configuration must match the installed binaries' MSVC runtime,
which vcpkg's own consumption provides automatically (both debug and release are built);
a plain-prefix consumer links release at the minimum.

On macOS the deployment floor is 13.3: export MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET before configuring,
or set VCPKG_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET in the triplet you build the package with.

On Linux the Wayland adapter builds against the host's Wayland, xkbcommon, Pango/Cairo and
D-Bus development packages. The libdecor frame and the Wayland protocol definitions are
vendored into the package, so no libdecor package, plugin directory or CMake option is
needed. The supported floor is RHEL/Alma 9 (glibc 2.34, libstdc++ 3.4.29, Wayland 1.21);
Ubuntu 24.04 is the current configuration. On Debian/Ubuntu install:

    sudo apt-get install libwayland-dev libxkbcommon-dev libpango1.0-dev libcairo2-dev libdbus-1-dev pkg-config

The portable CPU renderer is the Linux default until the Vulkan backend lands (#110).
